# The build context is the repository root: docker-compose.yml sets `context: ..`
# relative to deploy/, which on the server is /srv/vidar — the directory that
# also holds the live database, every monthly archive zip, and the production
# .env. None of that is needed to build the image.
#
# BuildKit (the default since Docker 23) only transfers files a COPY actually
# matches, so today it sends ~565 kB rather than the whole directory. This file
# is not there to fix that number; it is there so the guarantee does not depend
# on which builder ran. `DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0` still sends the entire context —
# measured at 47.95 MB in a local checkout, and on the server that figure is the
# database plus the archives. A single `COPY . .` added to the Dockerfile would
# put them in an image layer under either builder.

# Data and secrets — never part of an image, under any builder.
# The **/ prefix is load-bearing: dockerignore patterns are anchored to the
# context root, unlike gitignore's match-at-any-depth. A bare `.env` therefore
# missed deploy/.env — the very file docker-compose.yml tells the operator to
# create for NGINX_LOG_DIR/VIDAR_DATA_DIR.
data/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
.deploy.conf
.deploy.conf.bak

# History and development-only trees.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
tmp/
# docs/ itself is NOT excluded: the dashboard renders the .md files at
# /docs, so they ship. The screenshots do not — ~2.4 MB for the README on
# GitHub, and no .md references an image.
docs/img/
tests/
scripts/
.github/
# Root-level Markdown except the one that names the project. docs/*.md are not
# matched by this — the image serves them at /docs.
*.md
!README.md
pyproject.toml
requirements/dev.txt
node_modules/
package.json
package-lock.json
vitest.config.js
.pre-commit-config.yaml

# Build and editor droppings.
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg-info/
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
